# Content script and page-world

## Boundaries

- Content script and page-world are vanilla TypeScript — never mount React on x.com.
- Keep the Nostr secret key and signing imports out of these packages.
- Isolate injected UI with Shadow DOM; styles stay inside the shadow root.
- Do not modify X's existing requests. Do not modify X responses except the intentional timeline JSON rewrite (`json-trust-filter.ts`): allowlisted home/timeline GraphQL JSON may be filtered (hide-only) and optionally backfilled so X never mounts removed items — required for timeline render performance. Do not start GraphQL searches or other X account actions.

## DOM and SPA

- Prefer semantic post attributes and Schema.org metadata over generated classes; `data-testid` is a compatibility fallback only.
- Discover author name rows, chips, and badges through `scanner.ts` helpers (`findAuthorNameRow`, chip slots, verified badge) so ambient/chip mounts survive X markup churn. Keep lookups article-scoped for speed.
- X draws display name and handle differently per location. Each User chrome has its own mount slot (see `docs/x-page-chrome.md`). Do not reuse UserRail (`placeAfterDisplayNameIcons`) for UserAuthor (`ensureAuthorNameMetaMount`), or the reverse. Atoms are shared; slots are not.
- Make mounts idempotent and compatible with SPA navigation and dynamically inserted posts.
- ArticleScanner: do not reset an in-flight 180ms scan timer; watch `data-testid` so late tweet tags on profile UserTweets still mount. Same pattern as UserCell.
- Preserve X's existing behavior; do not intercept clicks or rewrite host UI outside the panel/chip hosts.

## Page-world → content bridge

- Passive observer: clone allowlisted successful JSON only; discard raw payloads after extracting public tuples.
- Timeline trust filter (exception): when hide filters are active, rewrite allowlisted timeline GraphQL response bodies in place (and backfill pages if the feed would be too short). Prefer fail-open (pass through the original body) on parse/rewrite failure. Keep rewrite scoped to hide/filter of timeline entries — do not invent posts, mutate non-timeline operations, or perform X account actions.
- Forward only validated, normalized records (`twitterId`, handle, post IDs, etc.) — never raw GraphQL bodies, cookies, or bearer tokens.
- Do not start an X search. Forward validated proof matches only when the post is already in an allowlisted response.
- Page↔content identity / proof / JSON-filter traffic uses a capability-bound `MessageChannel` (`page-world-bridge-protocol.ts`); do not put application payloads on public `window.postMessage` after handshake. Persist `post*` identity fields from the passive candidate. Do not request the post from X.

## Timeline CPU

- The X timeline and content/page-world scripts share one main thread. If this code makes the feed unresponsive, nothing else matters. See `docs/architecture.md` § Timeline CPU and responsiveness.
- Check every change here for scroll cost: no per-article RPC, no React on X, no extra MutationObserver or parse work without batching and debounce.

## Trust UI hot path

- Batch visible account IDs via `QUERY_TRUST_BATCH` and post IDs via `QUERY_RATING_BATCH` (roughly 20–50), not one RPC per cell.
- Debounce/coalesce with the existing scan timer (~180 ms).
- Do not read IndexedDB from the content script for trust answers — query the service worker.

## Build

- Content and page bundles are self-contained IIFEs (`vite.content.config.ts`, `vite.page.config.ts`).
- Content-script strings use `src/i18n/resources.ts` (`en`/`da`); see `internationalization.mdc`.
